     Well, ever since Nethack went overlay on PCs, it hasn't run in a
very good environment for skill.  I still claim that strategy is the
essential component.  I do partially retract my comments on luck, q.v.

:>     Horrors!  Why not just toss a die at the beginning of the game and
:>tell you whether you won or not.
:
:Luck has always been a part of any game that depends for most of its
:functioning on the output of a random number generator.  Remember:
:"Full moon, you feel lucky!"?  Taking luck out of the game destroys its
:essential nature.  It's perfectly fine to say "Curse my bad luck, my level
:20 character got killed by an entirely arbitrary trap.", it is quite another
:thing to say "Take all the luck out of this game so I never have to suffer."
     I certainly say that Nethack should include luck.  But I think at
no point except near the beginning (at the beginning, it's very hard to
make a good game balance for all characters, and, in addition, who
really cares if you lose 15 minutes of playing) should luck be the
deciding factor.  I think at all points you should be able to survive
given correct play (and not simply guessing the right option, for that
matter).

[1]:>     Of course, I'm performing my own fallacy.  I'm not denouncing all
:>occurances of randomness.  But I'm of the opinion that at no point
:>should a random factor, except early in the game, cause unavoidable
:>death to the player.  (e.g. "You step on a death trap!")
:
:Part of the essential nature of nethack is that at any point in the game,
:there are instances of bad luck awaiting the player, only capable of being
:overcome by _very_ skillful play.  Repeated playing develops such skills in
:response to perceived "bad luck".  The calls to remove luck from the game
:seem, to me, to be saying "I don't want to invest the play time to become
:skilled at this game; please trivialize the game for me instead."  The
:resulting game _would_ be trivial; where is the fun of a game you can win
:in a walk?
     Agreed.  I'm not calling to remove luck from that game, as stated
in [1].  I'm not sure whether this constitutes a partial retraction. 
But I claim the deciding factor should be strategy.  That's what makes
the game in my opinion.  You seem to claim it's simply losing.  I claim
it's winning or losing based on strategic play.  And yes, it should be
*very* difficult, I agree, but there is a difference between a luck
challenge and a strategy challenge.  A game that's difficult to win due
to luck can still be won, but it's not nearly so interesting as a game
that's difficult to win due to the necessity of creating an elaborate
strategy and following it.

:To the contrary, it is the excellent blend of strategy, luck, and skill that
:makes the game so addictive.  It seems to make carping and complaining also
:addictive, which is a pity, but maybe an _essential_ part of human nature, so
:I suppose to be consistent, I should suffer it in silence.  Not a chance!
     For the record, when I stated (paragraph omitted) that Nethack
should not be "a game of luck," I was referring to the deciding element,
not simply occurances.
     I wouldn't say I'm complaining.  I'm certainly making an argument,
but I've never lost a real game of any great magnitude (promising
games, yes, but nothing especially great) due to the things others are
saying here.  I've eaten a tin of spinach and choked on it without
confirmation, but I consider that strategy (as I was satiated), not
luck.  I also consider it deceptive, since I was previously under the
perfectly reasonable assumption that you would always get a confirm
question before choking unless you had gotten one recently.  It always
happened before, and it seemed to be the logical purpose of the
confirmation.

